"He does not yet speak, but understands tone, and remembers who fed him and who hurt him."
"He is ravenous for Qi, and the young rice grown near the willow holds a trace of it."
"By his wallow grow a few Moon-Dew Orchids, spirit herbs, pale blue and faintly glowing at night."
"One Moon-Dew Orchid would sell for many silver taels to anyone who knew what it was."
"Eaten raw, a Moon-Dew Orchid floods a mortal body with Qi it cannot hold."
"Brewed by one who knows herbs, it gently opens the meridians and eases sensing Qi."
"The river's Qi vein runs under the woods up from the willow, stirring as the hills' veins mend."
"More beasts will stir along that vein in the coming years, as the old wound in the land heals."
"Old Tusk will come back to the terraces by the willow within three nights, drawn by the rice."
"Fire and loud noise make Old Tusk wary but not afraid; a spear wound would enrage him."
"Old Tusk's core, hide and tusks would fetch many silver taels from any sect."
"Old Tusk is the torn-eared boar that killed Tie Shan, and still bears Tie Bo's spear scar."
"Mundane poison does not harm a spirit beast; aconite on a stake would be no more than dirt."
"Old Tusk's hide turns a mortal knife, arrow or spear; only a thrust into eye or throat can bite."
"His charge is what makes any stake lethal, and fire enrages rather than frightens him."
"Smoke can drive him off a wallow for a night, but not kill him, and he would remember it."
"He can leave a pit in one leap, being far stronger than his size suggests."
